UMD = Universal python package for post-processing Molecular Dynamics runs.

=====

UMD is a universal Python package for post-processing molecular dynamics runs. UMD is based on a generalized ASCII free format, the .umd.dat files, which store the atomic trajectories and the relevant thermodynamic information of the simulations. Parsers convert the outputs of VASP, QBox and LAMMPS into this format; the analysis scripts read it.
UMD includes two core libraries, one for the file formats (umd_processes_fast.py, with its C reader) and one for crystallography (crystallography.py).
UMD allows for computation of:
- structural properties: pair distribution functions and coordination numbers, bonds, polymerization, chemical speciation, interatomic angles, species and bond lifetimes, liquid/vapor partitioning
- transport properties: mean square displacements of atomic types, of individual atoms, of chemical species, along any axes; diffusion coefficients and the diffusion tensor, with errors; viscosity
- thermodynamic properties: averages of the thermodynamic quantities with their errors, heat capacity, velocity autocorrelation functions and vibrational densities of states, harmonic and two-phase (2PT) thermodynamics, quantum corrections, free energies and entropies by thermodynamic integration
It also checks the quality of a run, builds new structures (supercells, shuffled, inserted, scaled, substituted), and converts UMD files to extended XYZ, VASP POSCAR, LAMMPS data and dump, and DeepMD training sets.
The package is available via the github website as an open-access package and we encourage further collaborators.

